<section class="section" id="ci-gate">
  <h2>It runs on your pull requests</h2>
  <p>
    A principle nobody enforces is a slogan. The way corral enforces this one is
    a <strong>GitHub Action</strong>: it installs itself, audits only the files
    a pull request actually touched, and writes the verdict to the run page <em>verbatim</em> — including the lines that say what a number does <em>not</em> mean. One job, and there is no <code>corral</code> for you to
    install (the Action builds it with the runner's Go toolchain, which <code>ubuntu-latest</code> already has; a self-hosted runner needs <code>actions/setup-go</code> first). corral has no default models, so the job names every seat:
  </p>

  <pre class="yml"><code># one job of a workflow on: pull_request, runs-on: ubuntu-latest
permissions:
  id-token: write             # these three are required by attest
  attestations: write
  contents: read
steps:
  - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    with:
      fetch-depth: 0          # required — the diff needs a merge base
  - uses: pdbethke/corralai@v1.0.0-rc.24
    with:
      test-command: "go test ./..."
      derive-model: claude-sonnet-5
      writer-model: claude-sonnet-5
      mutant-model: claude-sonnet-5
      critic-model: claude-haiku-4-5    # must differ from the writer; "off" drops it
      anthropic-key: $&#123;&#123; secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY &#125;&#125;
      max-proven-missed: "0"  # fail on a DEMONSTRATED gap
      attest: "true"          # publish a receipt anyone can verify</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>max-proven-missed</code> rather than a kill-rate threshold, and the
    difference matters. A kill rate is a proportion of <em>freshly generated</em> faults, so it moves between runs on code nobody touched — we have watched an
    unchanged file score 0.375 and then 0.525, and a deliberately weakened suite
    score <em>higher</em> than it had a moment earlier. Set a bar near a healthy
    value and it reddens good work with nothing to act on. A <strong>proven gap</strong> is a survivor the herd then
    killed with a test it wrote and ran — a specific planted fault your suite misses,
    with a test shown to catch it. It is a gap in the tests, not a bug found in
    your code, and a person still decides whether the test belongs.
    So a <code>max-proven-missed</code> gate fails only with a concrete fault and
    test in hand. It does not catch everything: a fresh run regenerates its faults,
    so discovery can vary even on unchanged code, and a fresh run can miss a gap an
    earlier run found. The recorded fault and test stay inspectable; to compare test
    changes against the same faults, replay them locally
    with <code>corral certify --mutants</code> (the Action has no input for that).
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>attest</code> publishes the verdict as a signed in-toto statement
    through GitHub's attestation API, keyless, chained to the repository and
    workflow rather than to a key on a disposable runner. A reviewer verifies it
    without trusting the log, or you:
  </p>

  <pre class="yml"><code>gh attestation verify corral-audit-statement.json --repo &lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>
    It is written <em>before</em> the gate's exit code is honoured, so a failing
    audit still leaves a receipt. A verdict you only keep when it flatters you is
    not evidence.
  </p>

  <div class="receipt">
    <h3>What it said about this repository</h3>
    <p>
      We pointed it at the file that starts our own program, <code>cmd/corral/main.go</code>, as a gated check on a real commit. It
      planted <strong>40</strong> deliberate faults. Our suite caught <strong>10</strong> and slept through <strong>30</strong> — a kill rate of <strong>0.25</strong>. Then the test-writer authored a test we hadn't
      written and used it to kill one of those survivors, so that gap is
      demonstrated rather than alleged. The whole run took <strong>11m12s</strong> on a 2-core hosted runner.
    </p>
    <p class="aside">
      That is a bad number, published by the tool that produced it, on a run we
      could not quietly not-publish. A gate its own author can talk his way past
      is furniture.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tech-grid">
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>Scoped to the diff, on purpose</h3>
      <p>
        An audit costs roughly <em>(mutants × your suite's whole runtime)</em> per file — it scales with how long your tests take and how many files
        the PR touched, not with the size of the change. So the default is the
        PR's diff, <code>top</code> bounds how many files one run can spend on,
        and a whole-repo scan is something you opt into deliberately.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/github-action/">Wiring it up →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>Teeth are opt-in, and per file</h3>
      <p>
        A low kill rate alone does not fail a graded scan unless you set <code>min-kill-rate</code>. Set it and any single
        file below the threshold fails the run; a well-tested file elsewhere in
        the PR cannot average out a weak one. <code>max-proven-missed</code> is a
        separate gate on demonstrated test gaps; other execution failures can
        also fail the run. The thresholds are the operator's.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/github-action/">Exit codes &amp; thresholds →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="tech">
      <h3>It won't spend your money on a stranger</h3>
      <p>
        The bill lands on the repository that runs the workflow — your runner
        minutes, your API key. GitHub withholds secrets from fork pull requests:
        without a job guard they fail for want of a key, rather than skipping.
        The full workflow in the docs adds a same-repository and audit-label
        guard; the job excerpt above does not. Add that guard if you want
        paid runs to require an explicit label.
        Reaching for <code>pull_request_target</code> to "fix" that would run a
        contributor's code <em>with</em> your secrets.
      </p>
      <a href="/docs/github-action/">Who pays →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

